---
import BaseLayout from '../../components/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { toolsByCategory } from '../../lib/tools';
import { collectionPageGraph } from '../../lib/schema';
import { siteConfig } from '../../config/site';

const tools = toolsByCategory('reference');
const title = 'Fiber Arts Reference Charts';
const description =
  'CYC yarn weight charts, needle and hook size converters, Aida fabric sizes, and quilt dimensions—free FiberCalc reference tables.';
const jsonLd = collectionPageGraph({
  name: title,
  description,
  path: '/reference/',
  crumbs: [
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Reference', path: '/reference/' },
  ],
});
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/reference/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Reference' }]} />
    <h1>Reference &amp; converters</h1>
    <p class="takeaway">
      <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Use these charts when a ball band or pattern disagrees with your notions—for example,
      a US 8 knitting needle is 5.0 mm (UK 6) on the CYC-aligned converter.
    </p>
    <p class="meta-row"><span>Last reviewed {siteConfig.lastReviewed}</span></p>
    <div class="tool-grid">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <a class="tool-link" href={t.path}><h2>{t.title}</h2><p>{t.description}</p></a>
      ))}
      <a class="tool-link" href="/reference/yarn-weights/"><h2>CYC yarn weight standards</h2><p>Categories 0–7 with gauges and sources.</p></a>
      <a class="tool-link" href="/reference/quilt-sizes/"><h2>Quilt &amp; mattress sizes</h2><p>Standard blanket, quilt, and mattress dimensions.</p></a>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What belongs in Reference</h2>
      <p>
        Reference pages hold standards and conversion tables—not project estimators. Start with the
        <a href="/reference/yarn-weights/">CYC yarn weight standards</a> when substituting yarn or reading a ball-band
        icon. Use the <a href="/tools/needle-hook-converter/">needle &amp; hook converter</a> for US / metric / UK labels
        (metric millimeters are the reliable cross-brand measure; UK labels are approximate and not published by CYC).
        Cross-stitchers should open the <a href="/tools/cross-stitch-fabric-calculator/">Aida fabric calculator</a>.
        Quilters comparing tops to mattresses can use <a href="/reference/quilt-sizes/">quilt &amp; mattress sizes</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read CYC yarn categories</h2>
      <p>
        Craft Yarn Council categories run from 0 (Lace) through 7 (Jumbo). Category 4 (Medium / worsted) is the default
        in most FiberCalc yarn tools. Match the symbol on the ball band to the “#” column, then confirm the published
        knit or crochet gauge range. Wraps-per-inch (WPI) helps when a leftover cake has no label—see the CYC
        <a href="https://www.craftyarncouncil.com/standards/how-measure-wraps-inch-wpi" rel="noopener noreferrer">how to measure WPI</a>
        page alongside our table. Lace (category 0) crochet gauge on CYC is listed in double crochets, not single crochet.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked example: needle conversion</h2>
      <p>
        A US pattern calls for size 8 needles and your metric set is unmarked by US number. On the converter, US 8 maps
        to <strong>5.0 mm</strong> (commonly UK 6). Always swatch: letter/number systems vary slightly by brand, which is
        why CYC emphasizes the millimeter marking on the package.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked example: Aida cut size</h2>
      <p>
        A chart 140 stitches wide on 14-count Aida is 140 ÷ 14 = 10″ of design width. With 3″ margins on each side, cut
        fabric about <strong>16″</strong> wide. The Aida calculator also handles height and “over two” linen equivalents.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Trusting “DK” or “worsted” brand names without checking the CYC category icon and gauge.</li>
        <li>Mixing US letter hooks with steel-hook numbering (steel sizes run the opposite direction).</li>
        <li>Using mattress dimensions as quilt-top sizes without adding drop, tuck, or border ease.</li>
        <li>Treating FiberCalc yards-per-100 g midpoints as CYC standards—they are planning heuristics for substitution math.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related hubs and guides</h2>
      <p>
        Project calculators live under <a href="/knitting-crochet/">knitting &amp; crochet</a>,
        <a href="/sewing/">sewing</a>, and <a href="/quilting/">quilting</a>. Guides that use these charts:
        <a href="/guides/yarn-substitution-basics/">yarn substitution basics</a>,
        <a href="/guides/crochet-hook-size-conversion-chart/">hook size conversion chart</a>, and
        <a href="/guides/what-size-aida-for-cross-stitch/">what size Aida for cross stitch</a>. Source policy and density
        factors: <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
